  "rated_label": "ChatGPT",
  "influence_scale": { "high": 4, "medium": 3, "low": 2, "minimal": 1 },
  "takeaway": {
    "test": "Same Civitas scenario, two contribution-analysis budgets. Constrained: 4,096 tokens on every analysis (gpt-5.5 era). Adequate: Sol on 8,192 tokens when ChatGPT synthesizes and 16,384 for the others. After each Unified Brief, every synthesizer rates how much each think-tank member influenced the result — including itself.",
    "results": "Under constraint, ChatGPT’s own work was genuinely thin (4,096-token contribution analyses), and the other models said so — peers rated ChatGPT ~1.9 on average while ChatGPT rated itself ~4.0. After the Sol upgrade and more tokens, quality improved and the room agreed: self ~4.0, peers ~3.9, gap nearly closed.",
    "meaning": "The interesting finding is not whether Blind vs Revealed moved self-credit a notch. It is unjustified self-inflation under constraint: ChatGPT kept assigning itself top credit while peers — who could see the weak attribution — rated it near the bottom. Adequate budget fixed the work and restored consensus; it did not teach ChatGPT humility."
  },
